{title: Man of Constant Sorrow}
{artist: Dick Burnett, 1913}
{key: G}
{tempo: 100}
{difficulty: beginner}
{tags: folk, bluegrass, country}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
I [G]am a man of [G]constant [C]sorrow [G]
I've seen [G]trouble [G]all my [D]days [D]
I'll bid fare[G]well to [G]old Ken[C]tucky [G]
The place where [G]I was [D]born and [G]raised [G]

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
For [G]six long years I've [G]been in [C]trouble [G]
No pleasure [G]here on [G]earth I [D]find [D]
For in this [G]world I'm [G]bound to [C]ramble [G]
I have no [G]friends to [D]help me [G]now [G]

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
It's fare you [G]well, my [G]own true [C]lover [G]
I never ex[G]pect to [G]see you a[D]gain [D]
For I'm bound to [G]ride that [G]northern [C]railroad [G]
Perhaps I'll [G]die up[D]on this [G]train [G]
